+# Phase 1 Contract: `logredact` Command-Line Interface
+
+**Feature**: [spec.md](../spec.md) | **Plan**: [plan.md](../plan.md) | **Data model**:
+[data-model.md](../data-model.md)
+
+The command line is this project's only external interface. There is no library API promised to
+third parties, no network surface, and no file output. This document is the contract a test can
+be written against directly; entity rules are referenced from
+[data-model.md](../data-model.md) rather than restated.
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH` (spec Assumptions). No configuration file, environment variable,
+or network access affects behaviour (SC-007).
+
+## Argument grammar
+
+| Token | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `--count` | Enable the tally line. Accepted **in any position**, before, between, or after FILE arguments (FR-001). Repeating it is equivalent to giving it once. |
+| `-` | An ordinary file path named `-`. Not a stdin convention (spec Assumptions). |
+| any other token starting with `-` and longer than one character | Unrecognised option → usage error (FR-024). This includes `-h` and `--help`, which are deliberately not implemented (research D-007). |
+| anything else | A FILE path, consumed in the order given (FR-003). |
+
+Parsing is hand-written rather than `argparse`-based, because `argparse` cannot satisfy FR-001;
+see research D-007 for the evidence.
+
+## Streams
+
+| Stream | Content |
+|---|---|
+| **stdin** | Read **only** when no FILE argument is given (FR-002). With at least one FILE, stdin is never opened (FR-003). |
+| **stdout** | Every input line, redacted, concatenated in source order, line order preserved within each source (FR-004, FR-005). Nothing else — no banners, progress, or summaries. |
+| **stderr** | The `--count` tally line (FR-019), and diagnostics (FR-022, FR-024). Empty on a successful run without `--count` (FR-021). |
+
+Both text streams are UTF-8 with newline translation disabled, so terminators round-trip exactly
+(research D-008).
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+| Status | Condition |
+|---|---|
+| `0` | All requested input was read successfully (FR-025). |
+| `2` | A FILE could not be read (FR-023) **or** an unrecognised option was given (FR-024). The two are distinguished by the message, not the status. |
+| `141` (`128+SIGPIPE`) | stdout was closed early, e.g. `| head`. Terminates the way `cat` does, with no traceback and nothing on stderr (research D-010). |
+
+## Behavioural contract
+
+### C-1 — Redaction (FR-007…FR-018)
+
+Emails → `[EMAIL]`, IPv4 addresses → `[IP]`, Luhn-valid 13-19 digit card numbers (with their
+internal separators) → `[CARD]`. Recognition criteria, precedence, and the boundary rules are
+specified in [data-model.md §3](../data-model.md#3-redaction-rule). Everything else passes
+through unchanged, byte for byte (FR-006).
+
+### C-2 — Tally output (FR-019…FR-021)
+
+With `--count`, exactly one line is written to stderr **after all stdout output**:
+
+```text
+emails=N ips=N cards=N
+```
+
+One tally per run across all sources combined; occurrences, not distinct values; `0` when none
+(FR-020). Suppressed on the exit-2 path (FR-023).
+
+### C-3 — Diagnostics (FR-022, FR-024)
+
+Read failure, one line to stderr, naming the path and the reason:
+
+```text
+logredact: {path}: {reason}
+```
+
+e.g. `logredact: missing.log: No such file or directory`,
+`logredact: /tmp: Is a directory`, `logredact: secret.log: Permission denied`, and for
+undecodable bytes `logredact: binary.log: 'utf-8' codec can't decode byte 0xff in position 0:
+invalid start byte` (the reason text comes from the OS or the decoder, so tests assert the path
+and a stable substring rather than the full string — research D-009).
+
+Unrecognised option, to stderr, with nothing read and nothing written to stdout:
+
+```text
+usage: python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]
+logredact: unrecognized option: {token}
+```
+
+### C-4 — Failure sequencing (FR-023)
+
+Processing stops at the first unreadable FILE. Later FILE arguments are not opened. Output
+already written to stdout is left in place — it is already-sanitised data and is not retracted
+(spec Assumptions). No tally is printed. Exit status is 2.
+
+## Worked examples (normative — these are the SC-001 cases)
+
+Each block shows the exact streams and status. `$` lines are the command; `2>` marks stderr.
+
+**E-1 — stdin, mixed values**
+
+```console
+$ echo 'user jane@example.com from 10.0.0.7' | python -m logredact
+user [EMAIL] from [IP]
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+**E-2 — grouped card redacted, Luhn-invalid run preserved**
+
+```console
+$ echo 'paid with 4111 1111 1111 1111, ref 1234567890123' | python -m logredact
+paid with [CARD], ref 1234567890123
+```
+
+**E-3 — tally on stderr, output unaffected**
+
+```console
+$ echo 'a@b.io 1.2.3.4' | python -m logredact --count
+[EMAIL] [IP]
+2> emails=1 ips=1 cards=0
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+**E-4 — files in argument order, stdin ignored**
+
+```console
+$ python -m logredact a.log b.log < ignored.log
+<redacted a.log, then redacted b.log>
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+**E-5 — unreadable file mid-list**
+
+```console
+$ python -m logredact a.log missing.log --count
+<redacted a.log — already written>
+2> logredact: missing.log: No such file or directory
+$ echo $?
+2
+```
+
+No tally line, and `--count` does not change that (FR-023).
+
+**E-6 — unrecognised option**
+
+```console
+$ python -m logredact --verbose a.log
+2> usage: python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]
+2> logredact: unrecognized option: --verbose
+$ echo $?
+2
+```
+
+stdout is empty; `a.log` is never opened (FR-024).
+
+**E-7 — closed output**
+
+```console
+$ python -m logredact big.log | head -2
+<first two redacted lines>
+$ echo ${PIPESTATUS[0]}
+141
+```
+
+stderr is empty — no traceback (research D-010).
+
+## Out of scope for this contract
+
+No `--help`, no in-place editing, no backups, no log file of its own, no configurable
+placeholders or patterns, no `-` stdin convention, no IPv6, phone number, name, URL, or API-key
+redaction, no structured-log awareness, no pseudonymisation, no allowlisting (spec Assumptions
+and "Out of scope").
